#. Tag: title
#: index.docbook:73
#, no-c-format
msgid "Introduction"
msgstr ""

#. Tag: title
#: index.docbook:76
#, no-c-format
msgid "<title>&appname;</title>"
msgstr ""

#. Tag: para
#: index.docbook:78
#, no-c-format
msgid ""
"&appname; is an application for managing your personal finances. It can keep "
"track of your incomes and expenses across several accounts, in several "
"currencies. It has all the features you should expect from such a tool, such "
"as categories, scheduled operations, graphical reporting, stocks "
"management... It also has some less common features, like fast operation "
"edition, search as you type, refund trackers, customizable attributes..."
msgstr ""

#. Tag: screeninfo
#: index.docbook:83
#, no-c-format
msgid ""
"<screeninfo>&appname; displaying pie charts of expenses per category</"
"screeninfo>"
msgstr ""

#. Tag: phrase
#: index.docbook:88
#, no-c-format
msgid ""
"<phrase>&appname; displaying pie charts of expenses per category</phrase>"
msgstr ""

#. Tag: title
#: index.docbook:94
#, no-c-format
msgid "What &appname; Is"
msgstr ""

#. Tag: para
#: index.docbook:96
#, no-c-format
msgid ""
"&appname; is a personal finances manager for &kde; 4. It is intended to be "
"used by individuals who want to keep track of their incomes, expenses and "
"investments."
msgstr ""

#. Tag: title
#: index.docbook:103
#, no-c-format
msgid "What &appname; Is Not"
msgstr ""

#. Tag: para
#: index.docbook:105
#, no-c-format
msgid ""
"&appname; is not a professional tool. It doesn't have functions expected "
"from a software you would use to run a small business. It doesn't manage "
"taxes, doesn't use double-entry system, or any advanced financial function."
msgstr ""

#. Tag: para
#: index.docbook:108
#, no-c-format
msgid ""
"If you were looking for such a tool, you may want to look at Kmymoney or "
"Kraft instead."
msgstr ""

#. Tag: title
#: index.docbook:114
#, no-c-format
msgid "Terminology"
msgstr ""

#. Tag: title
#: index.docbook:117
#, no-c-format
msgid "Document"
msgstr ""

#. Tag: para
#: index.docbook:118
#, no-c-format
msgid ""
"A document is a &appname; file (extension .skg). It can contain an "
"indefinite number of <link linkend=\"terminology-account\">accounts</link>. "
"You can use one document to manage all your accounts at once."
msgstr ""

#. Tag: title
#: index.docbook:124 index.docbook:720
#, no-c-format
msgid "<title>Account</title>"
msgstr ""

#. Tag: para
#: index.docbook:125
#, no-c-format
msgid ""
"A &appname; account is similar to your bank account. For example, if you own "
"one account for you, one for your wife, and one in common, you can create "
"these three accounts in &appname;. Every time you spend or earn some money, "
"you register an <link linkend=\"terminology-operation\">operation</link>, "
"and indicate the account this operation was made on."
msgstr ""

#. Tag: title
#: index.docbook:130
#, no-c-format
msgid "Operation"
msgstr ""

#. Tag: para
#: index.docbook:131
#, no-c-format
msgid ""
"An operation is either a credit or a debt on one of your accounts. By "
"affecting a <link linkend=\"terminology-category\">category</link> to an "
"operation, you are able to analyze how you spend your money."
msgstr ""

#. Tag: title
#: index.docbook:135
#, no-c-format
msgid "Standard Operation"
msgstr ""

#. Tag: para
#: index.docbook:136
#, no-c-format
msgid ""
"A standard operation is the basic operation in &appname;. Like \"Today, "
"spent 20€ in credit card for groceries\"."
msgstr ""

#. Tag: title
#: index.docbook:141 index.docbook:1423
#, no-c-format
msgid "Split Operation"
msgstr ""

#. Tag: para
#: index.docbook:142
#, no-c-format
msgid ""
"A split operation is an operation for which the total amount is split over "
"several <link linkend=\"terminology-category\">categories</link>, dates and/"
"or <link linkend=\"trackers\">trackers</link>."
msgstr ""

#. Tag: para
#: index.docbook:143
#, no-c-format
msgid ""
"For example, \"Yesterday, spent 100€ at the supermarket, of which 60€ were "
"for food, and 40€ for clothes\"."
msgstr ""

#. Tag: title
#: index.docbook:147 index.docbook:1448
#, no-c-format
msgid "Transfer"
msgstr ""

#. Tag: para
#: index.docbook:148
#, no-c-format
msgid ""
"A transfer in &appname; is equivalent to moving a quantity of money from one "
"of your &appname; accounts to another of your &appname; accounts."
msgstr ""

#. Tag: para
#: index.docbook:149
#, no-c-format
msgid ""
"This must not be confused with the payment mode chosen: if you made a "
"transfer, for example using your bank's website, to someone else's account, "
"this is not a transfer operation, because &appname; doesn't know about that "
"other person's account. It is a <link linkend="
"\"terminology_standard_operation\">standard operation</link> made with "
"transfer as a payment mode."
msgstr ""

#. Tag: para
#: index.docbook:150
#, no-c-format
msgid ""
"Read the <link linkend=\"transfer\">dedicated chapter</link> if things are "
"still unclear"
msgstr ""

#. Tag: title
#: index.docbook:154
#, no-c-format
msgid "Validated Operation"
msgstr ""

#. Tag: para
#: index.docbook:155
#, no-c-format
msgid ""
"When <link linkend=\"import\">Importing</link> operations, they are not "
"considered valid right away. You need first to manually validate them "
"before. A validated operation is this, an imported operation that has been "
"validated."
msgstr ""

#. Tag: para
#: index.docbook:156
#, no-c-format
msgid ""
"Note that it is possible to automatically validate operations on import, by "
"turning on the corresponding option in the settings."
msgstr ""

#. Tag: title
#: index.docbook:160
#, no-c-format
msgid "Pointed Operation"
msgstr ""

#. Tag: para
#: index.docbook:161
#, no-c-format
msgid ""
"During the <link linkend=\"reconciliation\">reconciliation process</link>, "
"you point in &appname; all operations that appear in the corresponding "
"account position. A pointed operation is one of these operations. It is not "
"<link linkend=\"terminology_checked_operation\">Checked</link> until the "
"reconciliation is completed."
msgstr ""

#. Tag: title
#: index.docbook:165
#, no-c-format
msgid "Checked Operation"
msgstr ""

#. Tag: para
#: index.docbook:166
#, no-c-format
msgid ""
"When all operations on your account's position have been pointed in "
"&appname;, you can complete the reconciliation process. All <link linkend="
"\"terminology_pointed_operation\">Pointed</link> operation are then turned "
"into Checked Operations."
msgstr ""

#. Tag: title
#: index.docbook:173
#, no-c-format
msgid "<title>Category</title>"
msgstr ""

#. Tag: para
#: index.docbook:174
#, no-c-format
msgid ""
"A category is basically a class of operations. Some classic examples include "
"\"Food\", \"Taxes\",\"Salary\"... A category can contain other categories: "
"it is a hierarchical structure. For example, category \"transport\" can "
"contain categories \"Bus\", \"Automotive\", \"plane\"... &appname; handles "
"an infinite depth of categories."
msgstr ""

